    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d685">

                <pb facs="0905" n="803" sameAs="frus:frus1935v01/pg803"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.00111 Armament Control/141</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chief of Naval Operations</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Standley</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">(SC)A 14–6</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1935-10-09">October 9,
                        1935</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: The following dispatch, received this date
                    from the Naval Attaché of the United States Embassy in Rome, is quoted for your
                    information: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“Request the following information relative to President’s Proclamation:
                            May visit and search of American merchant vessels be carried on on the
                            high seas by either belligerent? As a state of war has been proclaimed
                            may rights under laws of war be exercised?”</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <p>It is requested that this department be advised as to the nature of the reply to
                    be made to this dispatch.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>W. H. <hi rendition="#small-caps">Standley</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
